Most of a blog page is text. It doesn't need a JavaScript framework, and yet a typical single-page app ships one for every visitor. **Islands** flip the default: the page is static HTML, and only the components that need interactivity are hydrated.

![A page split into static regions and one interactive island](../../assets/posts/astro-islands-explained/islands-diagram.png)

## The mental model

Think of the page as an ocean of HTML with a few islands of JavaScript. Each island is independent: it loads its own code, on its own schedule.

```astro {% title="src/pages/index.astro" highlight="2,7" %}
---
import Search from '../components/Search.tsx';
const posts = await getPosts();
---
<Layout>
  <PostList posts={posts} />
  <Search client:visible />
</Layout>
```

{% callout type="tip" title="Pick the laziest directive that works" %}
`client:visible` waits until the component scrolls into view. For a search box in the footer, that often means it never loads at all.
{% /callout %}

## Directives at a glance

| Directive | Loads when | Good for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `client:load` | Immediately | Above-the-fold controls |
| `client:idle` | The browser is idle | Non-critical widgets |
| `client:visible` | It scrolls into view | Comments, search, embeds |
| `client:media` | A media query matches | Mobile-only menus |

